      "comments": "https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50411827",
      "image": null,
      "content": "Slack discovered that scaling multi-tenant software is rarely just an infrastructure problem; it is an architectural boundary problem. When Enterprise Grid launched in 2017, the fundamental assumption was that users lived almost entirely inside a single workspace.\n\nOver time, user behavior drifted far away from that assumption. Large enterprise customers began operating across dozens of distinct workspaces within the same organization. Client applications had to juggle multiple independent gateway connections, separate caching layers, and fragmented data stores just to render a unified view like unread messages or search results.\n\nInstead of patching performance cracks with heavier client-side aggregation or complicated caching hacks, Slack engineered Unified Grid. The re-architecture unifies cross-workspace data models at the backend layer, allowing desktop and mobile clients to establish a consolidated connection and treat an entire grid as a single coherent namespace.\n\nChallenging foundational domain assumptions in a mature system is risky, but it is often the only way to eliminate systemic complexity."
